# Deploy-time Direct Lake connection overrides (dataplatform-owned).
#
# The ontology hub authors `gold.direct_lake_connection` in its own `kairos.yaml`, and
# `emit-gold` bakes the DEFAULT environment's OneLake URL into the semantic model's TMDL.
# The hub also ships a `parameter.yml` whose `find_replace` entry rewrites that URL per
# environment at deploy time -- but its `replace_value` map can only cover environments
# the HUB itself declares.
#
# This file is that seam from the other side: it lets the dataplatform point a deployment
# at Fabric infrastructure the hub knows nothing about, so real workspace and item
# GUIDs never have to be committed to the hub repo.
#
# How it is applied (see .github/workflows/deploy-powerbi-semantic-model.yml):
#   - `find_value` is ALWAYS taken from the hub's shipped `parameter.yml`. It must match
#     the URL baked into the TMDL byte for byte; only the hub knows it, and overriding it
#     here would make the rewrite silently miss.
#   - Only `replace_value[<target_environment>]` is overridden, using the GUIDs below.
#   - Environments absent from this file keep whatever the hub declared.
#   - No file, or no matching environment => the hub's own values are used.
#   - The deploy fails if the target environment is declared neither here nor by the
#     hub: fabric-cicd would silently deploy the hub's default workspace.
#
# The archive and its verified SHA-256 are never modified; the rewrite happens on the
# extracted `parameter.yml` after verification, and the workflow logs before/after.
#
# Copy to `gold-connections.yml` (same directory). A whole value written as
# ${FABRIC_WORKSPACE_ID} or ${FABRIC_ITEM_ID} is resolved at deploy time from the
# repository VARIABLE (or secret) of that name -- the deploy workflow passes exactly those
# two into the override step, so no other ${NAME} can resolve. `item_id` is the Fabric
# item Gold lives in: for the fabric-warehouse adapter, the Warehouse dbt writes Gold into
# (`lakehouse_id` is still accepted as a deprecated alias). With the deploy job running in
# a GitHub Environment (DD-239), each Environment supplies its own two values, so one
# entry per environment name with the same two references is all this file needs.

environments:
  PROD:
    workspace_id: "${FABRIC_WORKSPACE_ID}"
    item_id: "${FABRIC_ITEM_ID}"
